  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThe slow moving upper level low currently overhead will gradually \ndissipate and be absorbed into the mean flow over the next couple of \ndays. During this transition, steady August moisture and daytime \nheating will allow scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms \nto fire off each afternoon. Mesoanalysis and forecast soundings \nindicate a relatively moist airmass with dewpoints in the upper 60s \nto low 70s helping to increase MLCAPE values, generally ranging from \n1000 to 2000 J/kg. Given weak steering flow of generally 10 knots or \nless, slow moving storms will have the potential to drop a quick \ncouple of inches of rain over a short period. 1-hour flash flood \nguidance ranges from 1.5 to 2 inches across the foothills and East \nTennessee mountains, and 1.5 to 2.5 inches in southeast Tennessee \nand southwest North Carolina. Slow moving storms tracking over \nalready saturated soils or anchoring to terrain features will \npresent an isolated flash flood threat. Additionally, deep profiles \nand high freezing levels will support a mix of gusty winds, frequent \nlightning, and brief small hail with the stronger core structures. \n\nAs the upper low feature dampens out to end the week, orographically \nforced convection will become increasingly dominant over broader \nvalley based heating elements, though any lingering outflow \nboundaries could still spark secondary valley development. High \ntemperatures will hover near seasonal normals, generally in the \nmiddle to upper 80s for lower elevations.  \n\nHeading into the weekend and early next week, the remnants of the \nupper low phase completely into the mean flow aloft, while a \nnorthern stream trough drops into the Great Lakes region. This setup \nwill maintain weak upper level divergence across the southern \nAppalachians. Diurnal heating combined with continuous deep layer \nmoisture will maintain daily chances of afternoon/evening \nconvection. By early next week, deterministic models diverge \nregarding synoptic details and low level jet enhancements with the \nGFS remaining more bullish on coverage compared to the drier ECMWF. \nNBM paints a broad 40-50% PoPs for much of the area in response. \nCIPS analogs suggest that any enhanced severe weather threat will \nremain displaced to our north and east closer to stronger synoptic \nforcing and deep-layer shear, leaving our area locked into typical \nlate summer diurnal pulse activity with heavy downpours as the \nprimary hazard.",
